# MONITORING-LOG-TIMESTAMP-ROLLOVER-1 — log timestamp authority

Extends [113-monitoring-logs-watch-truthfulness](113-monitoring-logs-watch-truthfulness.mdc)
and [114-monitoring-log-source-authority](114-monitoring-log-source-authority.mdc).
Rule 114 settled **which files** the monitor may read. This rule settles **what an
event's timestamp means** once a line has been read.

## R1 — Freshness derives from the timestamp the log event carries

An event's age is the distance between `now` and the timestamp embedded in that
event's own header line, interpreted per the canonical log timestamp format. It is
never derived from the file's `mtime`, never from the rotated filename's date, and
never from the position of the line in the file.

## R2 — A date the parser had to invent is not a date

`Carbon::parse()` is inconsistently permissive: it throws on some impossible
timestamps (`2026-13-01`, `2026-08-32`, hour `25`, minute `61`) but silently
*normalises* others onto a neighbouring calendar date. Any parsed value that does
not reproduce the exact calendar digits the line contained is **unageable** and
must take the null/undated path, not a fresh/historical bucket.

The check is a faithfulness test, not a format whitelist. Anything that round-trips
is accepted unchanged — Laravel's `Y-m-d H:i:s`, Monolog's ISO-8601-with-offset,
fractional seconds, explicit offsets. **Never replace it with a list of permitted
formats:** production writes one format today, and a whitelist that guessed wrong
would turn every real error into `undated` and produce a permanent, un-clearable
WATCH storm.

## R3 — Rollover moves timestamps in BOTH directions

Do not repeat the superseded claim that rollover "only ever moves a timestamp
later". It does not:

| digits | becomes | direction |
|---|---|---|
| `2026-02-30` | `2026-03-02` | forward |
| `2026-02-29` (non-leap) | `2026-03-01` | forward |
| `2026-08-00` | `2026-07-31` | **backward**, previous month |
| `2026-00-15` | `2025-12-15` | **backward, previous year** |

A backward roll carries an error out of the window into `historical`, where it stops
driving the verdict — a **false green**. A forward roll reports a fresh error and
publishes a `latest_fresh_error_at` for an instant that never occurred — a **false
WATCH** on fabricated evidence, which no operator can clear.

## R4 — The header's trailing segment is not free text

The trailing segment after the seconds field must not be handed to a permissive
parser as-is. PHP relative modifiers (`-1 year`, `+5 days`, `next monday`) are
honoured by `Carbon` and are an unbounded, caller-chosen age shift that otherwise
still reports `timestamp_parse_status: ok`. R2's round-trip closes this class,
because a modifier changes the digits.

## R5 — The observation window is inclusive of its own start

`event_timestamp >= window_start`, where `window_start = now - lookbackSeconds`.
An event at exactly `now - 24h` is **fresh**; at `now - 24h - 1s` it is historical.
Pin any change to this with the `now-23:59:59` / `now-24:00:00` / `now-24:00:01`
triple.

## R6 — Display timezone is not classification

Rendering a timestamp in WITA (or any reporting timezone) is presentation only. The
same instant must produce the same freshness verdict however it is written, and a
UTC-day / local-day disagreement must never move an event across the window
boundary.

## R7 — Rotation boundary ≠ observation boundary

A daily file's date boundary and the lookback window's boundary are independent. A
previous day's file that intersects the active window is still inspected in full
(rule 114). A timestamp fix must never reintroduce "current file only" logic.

## R8 — `ClinicalClock` is not the monitoring clock

Monitoring ages infrastructure events against `now()` in the application timezone.
`ClinicalClock` is the clinical business-date authority (Asia/Makassar) and must not
be wired into log ageing. The domains stay separate.

## R9 — Unageable and future events fail closed

An `ERROR` whose freshness cannot be determined must never be silently discarded and
must never leave the logs section reporting OK. `undated_error_like_count > 0`
forces `freshnessUndetermined` ⇒ WATCH, and OK is provably unreachable. A
future-dated error is evidence of clock or parser corruption and must stay counted.

## R10 — Rollover tests are deterministic

Every calendar-boundary test freezes the clock (`Carbon::setTestNow`). A test that
only fails in January, on a month end, or at midnight is not a regression test. The
suite is named to match the `PilotPerformanceSnapshot` token in **both** NSF-R011
critical-gate variants, so it runs on a real authoritative CI path.
